Fillets: the walk past an end of the spine stops before its sections get
singular (mitcad#121, with 0075).

Past the spine's ends the guide (ChFiDS_ElSpine) is extended by straight
lines, so that the blend reaches the faces that cut it there. A rolling
ball's centre does not follow that line: where the edge is curved, the
guide's normal planes turn away from the ball's section plane, and where a
plane comes tangent to the centre's curve the sections are singular (the
contacts run ever faster along the guide, the walk ends in tangency
points). No surface approximates the line through that (a rounding round a
cone and a cylinder on one axis, its extension 15 long on a circle of
radius 17, failed with "Failed approximation").

ChFi3d_CutSingularExtension, at the end of the walked blend's ComputeData
(after the walk is continued into the extensions): on an open spine, from
the last point within the spine outwards, the line is ended at the first
point where the contacts run twice as fast along the guide as at the
spine's end (the guide's plane has turned 60 degrees from the ball's), or
where the walk reached a tangency point. Within the spine nothing changes;
the extension that is kept still runs past the end.
